caption: Demon Lord Worked Example

You are a newly-christened Demon Lord, and after slaughtering a few locals, buying a soul-holding vessel, and gathering a couple of souls (and a bit of experience), you decide to make your first minions and let them loose on the world to get your passive exp flowing. You design a basic Imp racial template, costing only a quarter of the campaign’s starting value. A group of ten of those will cost you six character points. The random mortals you slew were worth average for non-combatant civilians; each one has about 25 character points, and you earn half a percent of that, or .125 character points. Put another way, every 8 kills is a character point. If you consume the soul, that’s another .125… so you go out, slaughter about 60 more mortals, and get enough experience to buy the ally group. You allocate ten of the souls you’ve collected to the minions, and your new imps are born. You send them out to slaughter and take a nap. Eight hours later, your minions have found a small village, slaughtered it, and earned another 80 kills. For each kill, the minion responsible earns .125 character points. They averaged 8 kills each, so one experience each. You earn one-half of all the exp your direct minions do, so you earn 5 exp.. Slightly more than enough to turn your small group of 10 imps to a small army of 50. As your army grows, so does your power.

Now that you have a steady supply of souls and experience, you think it is time to make some Coin! A Quintus is a Torment chamber with the Gadget limitation. Your mighty Will of 20 translates to a mass-per-point for your Afterlife of 8, meaning it costs a bit more than 500 points for the coin itself and another 20 for the torment. Quintus are unbreakable, but they can be stolen. Each soul is, technically, unique, but the Quintus themselves are fungible. This ends up in a -20% discount, meaning forging the Quintus costs you about 416 character points. If you put one of your random villager souls in one, you would get 25 experience every 20,000 years, or one every 800 years. A small trickle per soul, but once you had enough of them… a mighty passive income that might some day rival that of your army. Certainly, more sustainable.

A few centuries later, you have practically conquered your home planet. It had a population of 500,000,000 when you first awoke your demon seed. You have one hundred thousand demons at the lowest tier, one thousand lieutenants and ten demon generals. Those ten are the only ally group you have to pay for personally; the rest are ally groups of ally groups. You did, however, have to provide the souls for all of them, at least, in the sense that you used souls they harvested. Each of your Imps averages five kills every month or so. Assuming the average peasant still has 25 character points, that means each Imp earns about 7.5 character points a year. This means your imps overall earn 750,000 experience a year, earning 150,000 experience total for your lieutenants (Averaging to 750 experience each), 75 hundred for each of your generals, and 37,500 experience each year for you personally.

However, being curious about the potential, you have also taken one hundred thousand souls and minted them into quintus. Twenty Thousand pentacoin (you don’t call that, but still) earns you 25 experience per quintus per ten thousand years, or, put another way, 250 experience per year. Less in quantity, but far more sustainable.

Since you aren’t and can’t replenish the souls of your world, your demons will begin running out of souls if they aren’t being stolen from another divinity. If they aren’t, you’ll run out of souls in about 400 years (assuming you are incarnating or minting all of them). If they are, you are very likely to attract the attention of whatever god is making them.
